  "query_preview": "..As ridging aloft has a firm grasp on our weather over \nthe next few days, temperatures will continue to warm, peaking \nMonday for most of the forecast area when highs will be 101 to 107 \nfrom Tucson westward and 93 to 100 in the valleys south and east of \nTucson. Most locales will be in moderate HeatRisk with localized \nmajor HeatRisk over the western deserts Monday, where some record \nhighs are in jeopardy. \n\nAt the surface, high pressure building down the plains and front \nrange of the Rockies will result in a return to easterly winds after \nmidnight tonight and continuing Monday. These winds will peak during \nthe morning hours Monday with gusts of 25 to 35 mph from Tucson \neastward, with a focus on areas most susceptible to east winds and \nin the Upper Gila River Valley including Safford. Winds will quickly \ntaper off Monday afternoon. \n\nThe aforementioned surface east winds in combination with the \nridging aloft gradually shifting off to our east, resulting in a \nmore southerly wind component aloft will help transport slightly \nelevated levels of moisture starting Monday and continuing into \nTuesday and Wednesday. This will be enough for some cloud build-ups, \nespecially over the higher terrain to the east of Tucson. While PoPs \nremain in the single digits, wouldn't be surprised to see virga or \neven a dry lightning strike or two Tuesday or Wednesday east of \nTucson. This is something we'll be monitoring the trends for over \nthe next couple of days. \n\nMoisture will then get shunted to our east by Thursday and \ncontinuing into next weekend as westerly flow resume with a weather \nsystem passing off to our north. This will knock temperatures down a \nfew degrees mid to late week. \n\nOutside of gusty east winds Monday morning and potential for any \nvirga/outflows Tuesday/Wednesday, just expect typical afternoon \nbreezes this week.",
